          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In abstract generality, a
            %em differential cohomology diagram
            is the hexagonal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            formed by the two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+squares"} fracture squares
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+a+monad"} unit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"} counit
            of, respectively, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♭
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+type"} stable homotopy type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat E
            in the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} tangent cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            express
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat E
            as being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            refinement by differential form data
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ♭
                    %mi dR
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat_{dR} \hat E
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology+theory"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology theory
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} represented
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \coloneqq \Pi(\hat E)
          %p
            Historically, it had been shown in (
            %a{:href => "#SimonsSullivan07"}> Simons-Sullivan 07
            ) for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            and in (
            %a{:href => "#FreedLott10"}> Freed-Lott 10
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#SimonsSullivan08"} Simons-Sullivan 08
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
            that these cohomology theories are characterized as sitting in the middle of a hexagonal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            of interlocking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequences"} exact sequences
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            datum, and, on the other hand, how the special cases of trivial underlying classes equipped with differential form datum and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
            differential form data sit inside the differential cohomology classes.
          %p At this schematic conceptual level a differential cohomology diagram looks as follows (where all unlabelled arrows are meant to be read as “evident inclusions”);
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
